But I think there’s another memory type hiding here

Call it Negative-Space Memory.

Don’t store only what was present.

Store what we deliberately removed and what became possible because it wasn’t there.

Something like:

N=(\text{removed assumption},\text{newly accessible states})

That’s very different from ordinary memory.

Imagine we remove assumption A, and suddenly solutions S_4,S_9,S_{22} become reachable.

Pantheon learns:

A \dashv \{S_4,S_9,S_{22}\}

meaning approximately:

A was blocking access to this region.

Now emptiness has acquired information.

Next time Pantheon becomes stuck in a similar terrain, Metamap can retrieve:

Removing this kind of assumption previously opened productive territory.

That’s learning what not to carry.

